# Intro
A single, fixed-mass star at the center of a uniform, periodic glass-IC gas
box, with LW/FUV hyperbolic (Cattaneo/P1-relaxation) propagation on
(photoionization and radiation pressure off), field initialised to zero
everywhere. Checks that the propagated FUV/LW interstellar-radiation-field
energy density (`FUVSpecificEnergies`/`LWSpecificEnergies`) never travels
farther than its causal reach `r <= c_hyp*(t - t0)` (`t0` = the star's
`BirthTime`, 0 here), the defining, checkable feature that distinguishes
this hyperbolic scheme from a screened-diffusion equation (infinite signal
speed by construction). Run duration is short on purpose: the point is to
see the field's leading edge, not let it reach the steady state the sibling
`ISRFHyperbolicPropagation` example measures.

`c_hyp` is not itself a snapshot field; `isrf_causal_reach_check.py`
reconstructs it from the run's own `timesteps.txt` (the bulk gas's own
time-step) and each snapshot's own median smoothing length, using the exact
formula `src/feedback/GEAR/radiation_isrf.c` uses.

## Pinned-neighbour variant
`hot_particle_temperature_K` (env var, `run.sh`) sets the single gas
particle closest to the star to a different initial temperature than the
rest of the box, giving that one particle a much shorter hydro time-step
and hence, via `c_hyp_i = min(C_hyp*h_i/dt_i, c)`, a much larger `c_hyp`
than its neighbours -- mimicking a particle pinned short by a recent
cooling/feedback/`timestep_sync` event. Requires `SPH:initial_temperature:
0` (`run.sh` sets this automatically whenever `hot_particle_temperature_K`
is nonzero) so the per-particle `InternalEnergy` `makeIC.py` writes is not
overwritten uniformly at start-up. The heated particle's own real pressure
also perturbs its local SPH density (a genuine hydrodynamic effect, not a
radiation artifact); `isrf_causal_reach_check.py --hot-particle-id`
excludes that one particle from the bulk `h`/`dt` estimate and reports it
separately.

## Metallicity, cooling
Same as `ISRFHyperbolicPropagation`: `initial_metallicity` must stay above
0 (the dust screening length is undefined at `Z=0`), and
`GrackleCooling:disable_cooling_for_debugging: 1` isolates the propagated
field from any gas thermal response while still resolving `chemistry_data`.

# Configure
```
./configure --with-chemistry=GEAR_10 --with-feedback=GEAR --with-cooling=grackle_0 --with-stars=GEAR --with-sink=GEAR --with-star-formation=GEAR --with-tracers=GEAR --with-kernel=wendland-C2 --with-grackle=path/to/grackle
make -j
```

# Run
```
./run.sh
```
Standard causal-reach test: `./run.sh`. Pinned-neighbour variant:
`hot_particle_temperature_K=3e4 ./run.sh`. Override gas density
(`gas_density`), gas particle mass (`gas_mass`), star mass/type
(`star_mass`, `star_type`), resolution level (`level`), run duration and
stepping (`time_end`, `dt_max`, `delta_time`), bulk temperature
(`bulk_temperature_K`), metallicity (`initial_metallicity`), and thread
count (`n_threads`); see `run.sh` for defaults. `run_name=<dir> ./run.sh`
moves a run's outputs into `<dir>`.

# Checking
```
python3 isrf_causal_reach_check.py
python3 isrf_causal_reach_check.py --hot-particle-id $(cat hot_particle_id.txt)
```
`run.sh` runs this automatically. Prints, per snapshot and band, the
measured extent of the field against `c_hyp*(t-t0)` at three amplitude
levels (10%/1%/0.1% of that snapshot's own peak, the most permissive
"plateau" definition available for a 3-D point source -- there is no flat
plateau to compare against as there would be in a 1-D slab test), and gates
on both that extent and on negativity anywhere in the box (unlike the
Tier-1 steady-state check, negativity here is a hard failure: the
wavefront is exactly where the estimator's known weaknesses are worst).
Produces a comparison plot (`--output`).
